  "void_obs": {
    "__comment": "[VOID SCHEMA — the OBSERVATION] One row = one void seen by one scan. Columns are the eight the real SAT output carries (product owner 2026-08-13): base wafer id, base_x, base_y, inchip_x, inchip_y, gate, radius_x, radius_y. They say exactly two things — WHERE (which package, which stack layer, where inside the die) and HOW BIG (an elliptical extent). | 🔴 THE GRADE IS NEVER STORED. Pass/fail is `area > threshold` and the threshold is a RECIPE parameter, so a stored verdict makes history un-re-judgeable and drops a 5%-recipe FAIL into the same cell as a 10%-recipe FAIL. Area is π·rx·ry and is NOT a column either — a 'larger than X' query gets an EXPRESSION INDEX (server/migrations/add_void_schema_indexes.sql), not a materialised number. This is ROOT_DEFECTS' '박제' root and DEFECT_SCHEMA_PROPOSAL §2-bis rejects it by name. b_bn is what this table exists NOT to be: a grade with no geometry and no threshold recorded. | KEY = (run_uid, inchip_x, inchip_y). run_uid already encodes the package, the layer, the method and the time, so repeating them in the key would only lengthen it. 🔴 WHAT THIS KEY CANNOT DO: it does not re-identify a void ACROSS runs. Two scans of the same package that both find 'the same' void produce two rows, because voids are found by an algorithm and its centroid will not repeat to the bit. That is deliberate — an observation belongs to the scan that made it — but nobody should read a void's history across runs out of this table. | Within one run, two rows at the same centroid would silently merge into one upsert, so the PARSER refuses the later ones and names the collision (reason `duplicate_location`); the UNIQUE index on business_key_val is the same refusal enforced by the database. | base_wafer_id/base_x/base_y/stack_gate are ALSO stored here although run_uid encodes them: the grid read path (GET /tables/{t}/data) is per-table and never joins, so a row that cannot say where it is would be unreadable on its own. They cannot drift from run_uid because ONE function derives both (parsers/void_sat_format.compose_run_uid). | `unit` is per-row and not on inspection_run for the same reason: a number whose unit is only reachable by a join is a number the grid shows without one, and unit-less numeric columns silently mix two machines' values (proposal §3). Cost, named: nothing forbids two rows of one run disagreeing about the unit — the parser takes one declaration per file, so it cannot happen from ingestion, but it is not enforced. | NO map_key_columns — see inspection_run. A void is a point with continuous coordinates, not a cell in a grid.",
    "business_key": "void_uid",
    "composite_key_source": [
      "run_uid",
      "inchip_x",
      "inchip_y"
    ],
    "composite_key_separator": "|",
    "column_types": {
      "void_uid": "string",
      "run_uid": "string",
      "base_wafer_id": "string",
      "base_x": "number",
      "base_y": "number",
      "stack_gate": "number",
      "inchip_x": "number",
      "inchip_y": "number",
      "radius_x": "number",
      "radius_y": "number",
      "unit": "string",
      "work_id": "string"
    },
    "display_columns": [
      "void_uid",
      "run_uid",
      "base_wafer_id",
      "base_x",
      "base_y",
      "stack_gate",
      "inchip_x",
      "inchip_y",
      "radius_x",
      "radius_y",
      "unit",
      "work_id"
    ],
    "workspace_name": "void"
  },
  "wafer_id_status": {
    "__comment": "[TRACE FIXTURE] Per (lot, slot) wafer-id snapshots. DELIBERATELY SPARSE -- it records only a fraction of the real state changes (user: 'SPARSE 함'). The deltas live in lot_event; this table is a corroborating witness, never the primary source. Treating it as complete is precisely the mistake the fixture is built to catch.",
    "business_key": "wid_key",
    "composite_key_source": [
