      "step": "string",
      "eqp_id": "string",
      "start_time": "string",
      "end_time": "string",
      "result": "string",
      "eventtime": "string",
      "recipe_id": "string",
      "knobs": "string"
    },
    "display_columns": [
      "proc_id",
      "wafer_id",
      "lot",
      "slot",
      "lot_id",
      "slot_no",
      "step",
      "eqp_id",
      "start_time",
      "end_time",
      "result",
      "eventtime",
      "recipe_id",
      "knobs"
    ]
  },
  "dt_job_attribution": {
    "__comment": "[TRACE FIXTURE -- enrichment DERIVED table] One row per DT job; the worklist for inference #1 (confirm dt_lot/dt_slot). Rows are produced by the synthesized enrichment dedup chain from dt_log -- nothing ingests this table directly. | business_key is dt_job and decision_key is [dt_job], which satisfies the loader's contract (business_key IN decision_key). | THE TARGET COLUMNS ARE SUFFIXED '_confirmed' RATHER THAN REUSING dt_lot/dt_slot. A virtual join exposes them back onto dt_log, and a virtual column that COLLIDES with a real one merges absent-only -- so on the 10% of rows carrying a WRONG dt_lot the recorded wrong value would win and the confirmation would be invisible. Under distinct names the two sit side by side and the disagreement is on screen, which is the whole point of seeding wrong values. | A row is IN THE QUEUE while both target columns are blank. cell_count is a count aggregation ('count' is the only aggregation the v1 loader supports). | [2026-08-22] 이 선언이 «라이브 카탈로그에만» 없어서 이 테이블을 가리키는 선언 둘(enrichment dt_job_lot_slot_attribution · 가상조인 dt_inventory_confirmed_from_attribution)이 조용히 건너뛰어지고 있었다. 샘플의 선언을 그대로 되돌려 놓은 것이다.",
    "business_key": "dt_job",
    "column_types": {
      "dt_job": "string",
      "dt_lot_confirmed": "string",
      "dt_slot_confirmed": "string",
      "cell_count": "number"
    },
    "display_columns": [
      "dt_job",
      "dt_lot_confirmed",
      "dt_slot_confirmed",
      "cell_count"
    ]
  },
  "dt_transfer_log": {
    "__comment": "[2026-08-23] Die transfer events -- one row per die moved from a core wafer to a DT wafer. SPLIT OUT OF dt_log by owner ruling: the ledger source `transfer_event` reads its whole relation ordered by dt_cell_key, and 34,939 of dt_log's 36,344 rows are not transfers at all -- they carry no b_wx, so the cursor's very first row refused with 'entity identity value is missing after preparation' and the source could never advance. A table of its own leaves nothing to filter. | business_key is dt_cell_key so the owner's existing transfer_event declaration fits unchanged (subject core_wafer_id/c_wx/c_wy, target dt_job_id/b_wx/b_wy, occurred_at event_time) -- only its `relation` moves, and the OWNER makes that edit on screen. | dt_x/dt_y intentionally carry the same coordinate as b_wx/b_wy: dt_cell_key is spelled {dt_job}_{dt_x}_{dt_y}, which is dt_log's verified format (0 mismatches over 20,000 live rows). | Written by scripts/seed_syn_die_transfer.py.",
    "business_key": "dt_cell_key",
    "column_types": {
      "b_wx": "number",
      "b_wy": "number",
      "c_wx": "number",
      "c_wy": "number",
      "core_wafer_id": "string",
      "dt_cell_key": "string",
      "dt_job": "string",
      "dt_job_id": "string",
      "dt_x": "number",
      "dt_y": "number",
      "event_time": "string",
      "product": "string"
    },
    "display_columns": [
      "dt_cell_key",
      "event_time",
      "product",
      "dt_job_id",
      "b_wx",
      "b_wy",
      "core_wafer_id",
      "c_wx",
      "c_wy",
      "dt_job",
      "dt_x",
      "dt_y"
    ]
  },
  "eqp_event": {
    "__comment": "[2026-08-23] Tool events -- one row per thing that happened to a piece of equipment over a SPAN of time.  `kind` separates the two sorts: `incident` (something went wrong) and `pm` (planned maintenance).  They share this table because both are 'an event attached to a tool with a time span': identical shape, and splitting them would mean writing the same walk twice. | 🔴 THE SPAN IS THE POINT.  `started_at` .. `ended_at` is an interval and not an instant because OVERLAP with a wafer's step time is the only question either sort is asked -- an instant overlaps nothing.  For `incident` the interval must CONTAIN at least one real run on that tool; for `pm` it must sit in a GAP with real runs on BOTH sides, which is what makes it name a step in a trend rather than decorate one.  The seeder measures both against `ledger_events` and refuses a row that fails. | 🔴 THE NAMES ARE READ, NEVER INVENTED.  `eqp_id` may only hold equipment that already appears inside live `processed_with` payloads (SYN-BD-*, SYN-MLD-*, SYN-STK-*, SYN-DIF-01, SYN-PC-*, SYN-MI-*, SYN-DTQ-*, SYN-FE-*) and `chamber_id` only CH-A / CH-B, the sole two chambers anywhere in that payload.  The die-transfer fixture of 2026-08-22 became an island nobody could walk into precisely because its names shared not one character with a registered entity. | `eqp_id` is filled on EVERY row; `chamber_id` only on chamber-scoped ones.  An event always belongs to a tool and the chamber is a VALUE on it -- the same shape `processed_with` already uses, where `chamber` is payload and never an entity.  A subject bound to `eqp_id` therefore never meets a NULL identity, which is what stalled `transfer_event` on `dt_log`. | `started_at`/`ended_at` are `datetime`, not text: R5 of `audit_schema_canon` makes a world time declared `string` a violation, and an overlap predicate over text is right only while every writer spells the instant identically. | ⚠️ Equipment and Chamber are NOT ledger entities today -- the subject_type census holds Wafer, Lot, die, DTJob, Recipe, WaferLeg and nothing else -- so declaring a source over this relation means introducing an entity type.  That is the owner's decision, not this table's. | Written by scripts/seed_syn_incident_comment.py.",
    "business_key": "event_uid",
    "column_types": {
      "event_uid": "string",
      "kind": "string",
      "target_type": "string",
      "eqp_id": "string",
      "chamber_id": "string",
      "started_at": "datetime",
      "ended_at": "datetime",
      "severity": "string",
      "summary": "string",
      "operator": "string"
    },
    "display_columns": [
      "event_uid",
      "kind",
      "started_at",
      "ended_at",
      "target_type",
      "eqp_id",
      "chamber_id",
      "severity",
      "summary",
      "operator"
    ]
  },
  "entity_comment": {
    "__comment": "[2026-08-23] Free-text comments a person leaves on a lot or a wafer -- author, time, body.  With `eqp_event`, one of the two R&D candidate kinds that had NO data at all: comments appear in none of the ledger's eleven predicates and in no source table (`file_ingestion_checkpoints.note` is for ingestion and `audit_logs` is a system log). | 🔴 THE NAMES ARE READ, NEVER INVENTED.  `target_id` may only hold a lot or a wafer that already carries atoms; the seeder counts them in `ledger_events` and refuses any name whose count is zero. | `target_id` is filled on EVERY row and `target_type` says which kind it names (`Lot` or `Wafer`), so no row carries a NULL identity -- the failure that stalled `transfer_event` on `dt_log`. | ⚠️ OPEN, AND IT BELONGS TO THE OWNER: one relation holding two entity types cannot be declared as a SINGLE ledger source as things stand.  A mapping's `entity_type` is a literal and not a column, and the only row-exclusion mechanism (`__source_row_excluded`) is emitted by a preparer implementation -- the live config declares zero `source_preparers`.  So either this relation gets split in two, or a preparer excludes by `target_type`.  The shape below keeps BOTH doors open; it does not choose. | Written by scripts/seed_syn_incident_comment.py.",
    "business_key": "comment_uid",
    "column_types": {
      "comment_uid": "string",
      "target_type": "string",
      "target_id": "string",
      "author": "string",
      "commented_at": "datetime",
      "body": "string"
    },
    "display_columns": [
      "comment_uid",
      "commented_at",
      "target_type",
      "target_id",
      "author",
      "body"
    ]
  },
  "step_inspection_run": {
    "__comment": "[STEP INSPECTION - the DENOMINATOR] One row = one die looked at, at one process step. THE STEP IS A VALUE, NOT A TABLE: `step` holds 'core' or 'dt' and a third step later costs one value rather than a schema. The subject columns are exactly the ledger's `die@1` key -- mat_type, mat_id, x, y -- so the declaration that maps this is a column swap of `die_inspection` rather than a new shape. Deliberately NOT modelled on `inspection_run`, whose `base_wafer_id`/`base_x`/`base_y` name the bonded wafer in the column itself and would need a second table per step. | This table is what makes 'looked at, found nothing' countable: a row here with no matching row in `step_defect_obs` is `scanned`, which is the control side of the marking contract.",
    "business_key": "run_uid",
    "composite_key_source": [
      "step",
      "mat_type",
      "mat_id",
      "x",
      "y",
      "observed_at"
    ],
    "composite_key_separator": "|",
    "column_types": {
      "run_uid": "string",
      "step": "string",
      "mat_type": "string",
      "mat_id": "string",
      "x": "number",
      "y": "number",
      "recipe_id": "string",
      "eqp_id": "string",
      "observed_at": "datetime"
    },
    "display_columns": [
      "run_uid",
      "step",
      "mat_type",
      "mat_id",
      "x",
      "y",
      "recipe_id",
      "eqp_id",
      "observed_at"
    ]
  },
  "step_defect_obs": {
    "__comment": "[STEP DEFECT - the OBSERVATION] One row = one defect seen on one die at one step, WITH ITS POSITION INSIDE THE DIE. That is the point of this table: `core_defect_map` and `dt_map` carry a die-grid coordinate and nothing finer, so today a composite can only be drawn after bonding. With inchip here, the same question can be asked at the core and dt steps -- whether a hot spot inside the chip is already there upstream or only appears after bonding. | THE SIZE COLUMNS SAY WHICH WIDTH THEY MEAN. `void_obs` stores `radius_x/y` (half) and `delam_obs` stores `extent_x/y` (full); calling both 'size' loses the factor of two and a composite drawing a half width as a full one is wrong without saying so. These are FULL widths, in `unit`. | `val` is a verdict CODE ('P'/'F'/'D' in the existing maps) and is deliberately not a number: turning codes into integers to satisfy a numeric role leaves nobody able to say what a 1 meant.",
    "business_key": "obs_uid",
    "composite_key_source": [
      "run_uid",
      "inchip_x",
      "inchip_y"
    ],
    "composite_key_separator": "|",
    "column_types": {
      "obs_uid": "string",
      "run_uid": "string",
      "step": "string",
      "mat_type": "string",
      "mat_id": "string",
      "x": "number",
      "y": "number",
      "inchip_x": "number",
      "inchip_y": "number",
      "extent_x": "number",
      "extent_y": "number",
      "unit": "string",
      "val": "string"
    },
    "display_columns": [
      "obs_uid",
      "run_uid",
      "step",
      "mat_type",
      "mat_id",
      "x",
      "y",
      "inchip_x",
      "inchip_y",
      "extent_x",
      "extent_y",
      "unit",
      "val"
    ]
  },
  "void_obs_observed": {
    "__comment": "READ-ONLY VIEW: void_obs joined to inspection_run on run_uid, so a void observation carries the time it was actually inspected. void_obs itself has no time column, and created_at is the loader's clock rather than the inspection's. Join measured total: 103,729 of 103,729, run_uid one-to-one. Rollback: DROP VIEW void_obs_observed;",
    "business_key": "void_uid",
    "composite_key_source": [
      "run_uid",
      "inchip_x",
      "inchip_y"
    ],
    "composite_key_separator": "|",
    "column_types": {
      "void_uid": "string",
      "run_uid": "string",
      "base_wafer_id": "string",
      "base_x": "number",
      "base_y": "number",
      "stack_gate": "number",
      "inchip_x": "number",
      "inchip_y": "number",
