  "bonding_core_lot": {
    "__comment": "READ-ONLY VIEW: every (bonding wafer, core wafer) pair in bonding_log, 3,650 rows, resolved through core_wafer_map on (core_lot, core_slot) with the slot cast to int on both sides ('02' vs 2.0). No collapsing: a bonding wafer takes dies from as many as 30 core wafers and naming one of them would be false. Uses event_time, NOT eventtime (the latter is NULL in all 380,273 rows). 1,072 (wafer, lot) pairs have no map row and make no edge, by design. Rollback: DROP VIEW bonding_core_lot;",
    "column_types": {
      "base_id": "string",
      "core_wafer": "string",
      "core_slot": "number",
      "event_time": "datetime"
    },
    "composite_key_source": [
      "base_id",
      "core_wafer"
    ],
    "composite_key_separator": "|"
  },
  "bonding_core_die": {
    "__comment": "READ-ONLY VIEW, one row per bonded die: 371,593 rows = 371,593 distinct (base_id, bx, by). Widened from bonding_core_lot, which folded the same 380,273 log rows to 3,650 with DISTINCT and dropped every coordinate. NOT joined to core_wafer_map: that LEFT JOIN inflates to 6,444,693 and the fold that made it survivable is what lost the coordinates. Core travels on this row's own core_lot/core_slot/cx/cy, present on 93,118 (25.1%). dt_seat is dt_lot||'|'||dt_slot because mat_id takes ONE column. Rollback: DROP VIEW bonding_core_die;",
    "column_types": {
      "base_id": "string",
      "bx": "number",
      "by": "number",
      "dt_lot": "string",
      "dt_slot": "number",
      "dt_x": "number",
      "dt_y": "number",
      "dt_seat": "string",
      "core_lot": "string",
      "core_slot": "number",
      "cx": "number",
      "cy": "number",
      "event_time": "datetime",
      "core_seat": "string",
      "core_wafer": "string"
    },
    "composite_key_source": [
      "base_id",
      "bx",
      "by"
    ],
    "composite_key_separator": "|"
  },
  "lot_slot_move": {
    "__comment": "READ-ONLY VIEW, one row per seat-to-seat move: 135 rows. Two lot_event rows paired on wafer id after exploding slot_numbers and wafer_ids positionally; both directions unioned because 38 moves are recorded only by the receiving lot. 21 of 135 change slot -- the same 21 the ledger's own slot_map qualifiers give by a separate path. event_type is the RECORDING row's: the giving-side axis gave one move two types and invented 20 track_in moves that do not exist. THE KEY INCLUDES event_time -- the five move columns alone give 122, because 13 seat pairs move twice at different instants (split then merge). Rollback: DROP VIEW lot_slot_move;",
    "column_types": {
      "from_lot": "string",
      "from_slot": "string",
      "to_lot": "string",
      "to_slot": "string",
      "wafer": "string",
      "event_time": "datetime",
      "event_type": "string"
    },
    "composite_key_source": [
      "from_lot",
      "from_slot",
      "to_lot",
      "to_slot",
      "wafer",
      "event_time"
    ],
    "composite_key_separator": "|"
  },
  "bonding_die_from_core": {
    "__comment": "READ-ONLY VIEW: bonding_core_die filtered to the rows that resolved a core wafer. 18,545 rows = 18,545 distinct (bonded die, core die); no fold, just a WHERE. It exists as its own relation because a null identity value does not refuse a MOLECULE -- it raises SourcePreparationError and the whole SOURCE dies, which would have taken all 371,593 DT-seat edges with it. Measured by dry run, not assumed. Rollback: DROP VIEW bonding_die_from_core;",
    "column_types": {
      "base_id": "string",
      "bx": "number",
      "by": "number",
      "core_wafer": "string",
      "cx": "number",
      "cy": "number",
      "core_seat": "string",
      "core_lot": "string",
      "core_slot": "number",
      "event_time": "datetime"
    },
    "composite_key_source": [
      "base_id",
      "bx",
      "by",
      "core_wafer",
      "cx",
      "cy"
    ],
    "composite_key_separator": "|"
  },
  "process_param": {
    "__comment": "[ledger source] One row per (wafer, step, physical quantity). LONG form on purpose: the 31 parameter names measured in ledger_events_pre_rebuild are step-specific, so a wide table would be 31 mostly-null columns AND every value would be a dead end. As rows, `param` becomes a quantity@1 entity the walk can leave from. Filled from the preserved pre-rebuild ledger, not invented. Owner ruling 2026-08-28: source data goes in a table and the ledger comes from that.",
    "business_key": "param_id",
    "column_types": {
      "param_id": "string",
      "wafer_id": "string",
      "step": "string",
      "param": "string",
      "value": "number",
